119	 * A card that clears must always be a card Nxeon can charge again
120	 *
121	 * REAL INCIDENT (2026-08-20). A brand-new customer's VPS order (a real, PAID
122	 * $8.88 charge — Stripe live PaymentIntent pi_3U6cCsHxcLe3GonT0wFpSrcU) went
123	 * through `createOrderCheckout` at a moment when that function did not yet set
124	 * `customer` / `payment_intent_data.setup_future_usage`. The card cleared, the
125	 * server was provisioned, and the account was left with NO Stripe customer and
126	 * NO saved payment method — unrenewable by the billing sweep. The very next
127	 * commit (cf9f9cb) closed that gap on the order/game/host/email/topup paths;
128	 * this test is the regression guard so it can never reopen on ANY of them, and
129	 * it extends the same guard to `createResizeCheckout` / `createDiskExpandCheckout`
130	 * — an upgrade or disk expansion can just as easily be a customer's first-ever
131	 * card charge (e.g. their original order was fully covered by the wallet or a
132	 * 100%-off promo), and it must not be the one purchase this codebase forgets to
133	 * save a card for.
134	 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
